From: ThirdReality <dev@3reality.com>
Subject: [PATCH] fix(wifi,mmc): probe aml_wifi after the SDIO host; ack IRQ before NULL check

Two changes.  Neither touches the request-completion path.

1) Root cause of the intermittent "wlan0 missing" boot (fix).

   The differentiator between a good and a bad boot is purely the probe order
   of ffe05000.sdio versus aml_wifi.  Measured over 80 boots (60 reset, 20
   reboot) on v2.14.01.56/.57/.58:

     80x PASS -> ffe05000.sdio probes at 0.193 .. 0.336s, "sdio_host is NULL"
                 never printed
      3x FAIL -> ffe05000.sdio probes at 0.85 .. 0.88s, "sdio_host is NULL"
                 printed twice

   On a FAIL boot wifi_dt runs first.  Its probe blocks for ~543ms in
   msleep(100) + msleep(200) + msleep(200), which both completes the chip
   power-on and pushes the SDIO host's probe half a second later, so the whole
   power-on sequence finishes before the host exists:

     0.383  set_wifi_power(1) -> sdio_reinit()      [wifi_dt.c]
     0.385  Error: sdio_host is NULL                -> does nothing
     0.595  probe-tail set_power(1)                 -> chip is now powered
     0.803  probe-tail -> sdio_reinit()
     0.804  Error: sdio_host is NULL                -> does nothing
     0.853  ffe05000.sdio: meson_mmc_probe: probe start
     0.883  ###mmc1: Set sdio_host ###
            ... and mmc1 never prints again.

   That silence is the actual failure.  mmc_add_host() starts the host's first
   rescan right there, but the chip has been powered for ~290ms and is in the
   window where it neither answers CMD52 nor raises the controller's response
   timeout, so mmc_wait_for_req_done()'s bare wait_for_completion() never
   returns.  On a PASS boot the same first rescan runs while the chip is still
   unpowered, fails cleanly in ~25ms with -110, and the later sdio_reinit()
   from set_wifi_power(1) enumerates the card in ~54ms.

   Nine seconds later the WiFi driver notices ("sdio not probe, need set
   power") and retries through usb_power_control(), but its sdio_rescan() does
   flush_work() on the work that has been stuck since 0.88s, so insmod joins
   mmc_rescan in D state:

     kworker/3:0  D  Workqueue: events_freezable mmc_rescan
       wait_for_completion <- mmc_wait_for_req_done <- mmc_wait_for_cmd
       <- mmc_io_rw_direct <- mmc_sdio_init_card <- mmc_attach_sdio
     insmod       D  sdio_reinit+0xa4/0xfc <- ... <- aml_insmod [vlsicomm]

   Fix: export sdio_host_is_ready() and have wifi_dev_probe() return
   -EPROBE_DEFER until the host is registered.  This turns the racing order
   into a guaranteed one and reproduces the PASS timeline on every boot.
   wifi_dev_probe() already defers on the PWM controller (-517 on the first
   attempt, retried successfully), so the mechanism is proven on this driver.

   For reference, 5.10 gets the same ordering by accident: meson-pwm probes
   after meson-gx-mmc there, and aml_wifi depends on the PWM, so it is pushed
   behind the host.  In 6.6 the PWM became ready first and the accidental
   guard disappeared.  Checking sdio_host directly does not rely on any other
   driver's probe timing.

   Behaviour change worth stating: if ffe05000.sdio never probes, aml_wifi now
   defers until deferred_probe_timeout instead of running and failing.  WiFi
   cannot work in that situation either way.

2) meson_mmc_irq() acked the controller only after its host->cmd NULL check
   (correctness fix, independent of the above).

   The early "return IRQ_NONE" left a level-triggered interrupt asserted, so
   the handler was re-entered immediately and the CPU livelocked - seen as a
   completely dead console with sysrq-over-BREAK also unresponsive, at the
   exact moment an out-of-band completer had cleared host->cmd.  Ack first,
   then return IRQ_HANDLED.  Nothing in-tree completes out of band today, so
   this is latent, but it is what made an earlier software-watchdog attempt
   turn a survivable D-state hang into a hard lockup.

Applies on top of jethome-0003 and jethome-0008.
---
--- a/drivers/mmc/host/meson-gx-mmc.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/host/meson-gx-mmc.c
@@ -943,13 +943,20 @@
 			  irq_en, raw_status);
 		 return IRQ_NONE;
 	 }
  
+	 /*
+	  * Ack BEFORE the NULL check.  Returning IRQ_NONE without acking leaves
+	  * this level-triggered line asserted, so the handler is re-entered
+	  * immediately and the CPU livelocks - observed as a dead console with
+	  * sysrq-over-BREAK also unresponsive.  Any out-of-band completion of
+	  * host->cmd reaches this path, so ack first and report the interrupt as
+	  * handled rather than leaving it pending forever.
+	  */
+	 writel(status, host->regs + SD_EMMC_STATUS);
+ 
 	 if (WARN_ON(!host) || WARN_ON(!host->cmd))
-		 return IRQ_NONE;
- 
-	 /* ack all raised interrupts */
-	 writel(status, host->regs + SD_EMMC_STATUS);
+		 return IRQ_HANDLED;
  
 	 cmd = host->cmd;
 	 data = cmd->data;
 	 cmd->error = 0;
@@ -1407,8 +1414,31 @@
 	 if (!ret)
 		 pr_info("Error: delayed_work mmc_rescan() already idle!\n");
  }
  
+/*
+ * Has the SDIO host probed and registered itself yet?
+ *
+ * sdio_host is assigned at the end of meson_mmc_probe().  The Amlogic WiFi
+ * platform driver (drivers/amlogic/wifi/wifi_dt.c) powers the W155S1 and then
+ * calls sdio_reinit() to enumerate it.  If wifi_dt probes first, both of its
+ * sdio_reinit() calls find no host and silently do nothing, so the
+ * post-power-on rescan never happens.  Worse, when this host finally does
+ * probe, mmc_add_host() kicks off its first rescan while the chip is already
+ * powered but not yet answering, and the unbounded wait in
+ * mmc_wait_for_req_done() wedges mmc_rescan permanently (kworker and the
+ * insmod that flush_work()s it both end up in D state, wlan0 never appears).
+ *
+ * Measured over 80 boots: every PASS had this host probing before 0.34s,
+ * every FAIL had it at 0.85-0.88s.  Exporting this lets wifi_dt defer its
+ * probe so the ordering is guaranteed instead of raced for.
+ */
+bool sdio_host_is_ready(void)
+{
+	return sdio_host != NULL;
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL(sdio_host_is_ready);
+
  void sdio_reinit(void)
  {
  #if 1
 	 printk("[%s] \n", __func__);
--- a/drivers/amlogic/wifi/wifi_dt.c
+++ b/drivers/amlogic/wifi/wifi_dt.c
@@ -732,13 +732,28 @@
 	 return 0;
  }
  #endif
  
+/* provided by drivers/mmc/host/meson-gx-mmc.c */
+extern bool sdio_host_is_ready(void);
+
 static int wifi_dev_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 {
 	 int ret;
 
 	 pr_info("### wifi_dev_probe: START, device=%s ###\n", dev_name(&pdev->dev));
+	 /*
+	  * Defer until the SDIO host has probed - see sdio_host_is_ready() in
+	  * drivers/mmc/host/meson-gx-mmc.c for why the ordering matters.  Without
+	  * this, on roughly 1 boot in 10 this probe wins the race, its
+	  * sdio_reinit() calls find no host and do nothing, and the SDIO host's
+	  * own first rescan then lands in the window where the chip is powered but
+	  * not yet answering, which wedges mmc_rescan for good and loses wlan0.
+	  */
+	 if (!sdio_host_is_ready()) {
+	 	pr_info("aml_wifi: SDIO host not registered yet, deferring probe\n");
+	 	return -EPROBE_DEFER;
+	 }
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_OF
 	 struct wifi_plat_info *plat;
 	 const char *value;
